WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.534 --> 00:00:10.455
was over for the day.

2
00:00:10.975 --> 00:00:13.636
Will watched his friends open their umbrellas and head home.

3
00:00:14.296 --> 00:00:15.656
Will had forgotten his umbrella.

4
00:00:15.676 --> 00:00:18.337
"It's a good thing Dad can pick me up today.

5
00:00:18.957 --> 00:00:20.317
The weather is terrible!"

6
00:00:20.797 --> 00:00:22.538
he thought and sat on a bench in the hallway.

7
00:00:22.558 --> 00:00:23.778
"I wonder why."

8
00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:29.800
Will's science class was studying weather, so he started to read the chapter about storms while he waited.

9
00:00:30.812 --> 00:00:33.133
After only a few paragraphs, he fell asleep.

10
00:00:35.955 --> 00:00:37.956
A gust of wind made Will open his eyes.

11
00:00:38.576 --> 00:00:40.437
All around him was the wide-open sky!

12
00:00:41.358 --> 00:00:45.880
Glancing down, he realized he was in the basket of a hot air balloon, high above the ground.

13
00:00:47.961 --> 00:00:50.022
Suddenly Will's phone beeped loudly.

14
00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:53.224
He pulled QT out of his pocket and said, "Hi!

15
00:00:53.664 --> 00:00:55.225
The people down there look like bugs!"

16
00:00:55.245 --> 00:01:00.368
"But they'll get bigger if we start to drop," QT warned.

17
00:01:00.888 --> 00:01:03.791
"Quick, pull the silver handles down to start the burners!"

18
00:01:05.592 --> 00:01:09.215
As soon as Will pulled the handles, huge flames shot out above him.

19
00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:11.597
"I don't want to burn the balloon!"

20
00:01:11.997 --> 00:01:14.880
he yelled over the burners' noise, letting go of the handles.

21
00:01:16.261 --> 00:01:18.943
An image of a hot air balloon lit up QT's screen.

22
00:01:18.963 --> 00:01:22.386
"We need the burners to heat the air in the balloon.

23
00:01:22.887 --> 00:01:25.669
Hot air is lighter than the cold air outside the balloon.

24
00:01:26.189 --> 00:01:28.912
That makes the balloon float," QT explained.

25
00:01:29.888 --> 00:01:32.170
"Hot air rising is very powerful.

26
00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:34.332
It also creates the weather, you know!"

27
00:01:34.352 --> 00:01:36.674
"Really?" Will asked.

28
00:01:37.154 --> 00:01:38.315
"How does it create weather?"

29
00:01:38.335 --> 00:01:43.539
"The sun heats the planet, but some areas get hotter than others.

30
00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:49.964
So the air in the hot regions rises, and cooler air moves in to take its place.

31
00:01:50.545 --> 00:01:52.526
We call this air movement wind."

32
00:01:52.546 --> 00:01:55.169
"So that's what makes wind!"

33
00:01:55.669 --> 00:01:57.150
Will watched a cloud pass by.

34
00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:59.092
"Does hot air make clouds too?"

35
00:02:00.048 --> 00:02:00.809
"Not really.

36
00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:04.871
When the sun heats water, some of those particles evaporate.

37
00:02:05.531 --> 00:02:08.953
That means they turn into a gas, called water vapor.

38
00:02:09.533 --> 00:02:11.734
The water vapor rises with the hot air.

39
00:02:12.315 --> 00:02:13.996
But the air cools with time.

40
00:02:14.516 --> 00:02:19.319
When it's cold enough, the water vapor condenses, meaning it becomes liquid water again.

41
00:02:20.419 --> 00:02:24.221
But the tiny droplets are so light that they float, forming clouds."

42
00:02:25.362 --> 00:02:27.883
Will pointed to a low, gray cloud in the distance.

43
00:02:27.903 --> 00:02:29.184
"That's water?"

44
00:02:30.128 --> 00:02:34.772
"Yes. The cloud looks dark and gray because the water is blocking the sunlight.

45
00:02:35.332 --> 00:02:41.177
That feathery one high above us is actually made of ice crystals, not water, because it's much colder

46
00:02:41.257 --> 00:02:43.099
higher up," QT explained.

47
00:02:44.059 --> 00:02:48.723
Gazing at the clouds, Will commented, "I like the ones that look like cotton balls best.

48
00:02:49.183 --> 00:02:50.745
Their shapes are always changing."

49
00:02:52.166 --> 00:02:54.548
"That's exactly what makes them dangerous!

50
00:02:55.616 --> 00:03:02.260
That harmless-looking, little cloud can quickly become a huge thundercloud, over 12,000 meters tall!

51
00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.781
Look to your left, and you'll see what I mean."

52
00:03:05.482 --> 00:03:08.404
Will turned and saw a tall, dark cloud in the distance.

53
00:03:08.424 --> 00:03:10.785
"Wow! That's huge."

54
00:03:11.405 --> 00:03:13.667
An image of a cloud appeared on QT's screen.

55
00:03:13.687 --> 00:03:18.670
"Thunderclouds form when there's a lot of warm, humid air.

56
00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:22.452
First a puffy, little cloud appears, like that one.

57
00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:29.239
If the cloud stays warmer than the air around it, it grows taller; warm air keeps moving up

58
00:03:29.399 --> 00:03:29.759
and up.

59
00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:35.582
The water droplets combine with other droplets until they get too big and heavy.

60
00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:37.903
Then they fall to the ground.

61
00:03:38.543 --> 00:03:42.845
Snow and hail also fall when they get too heavy," QT explained.

62
00:03:42.865 --> 00:03:45.306
"It's raining over there!

63
00:03:45.806 --> 00:03:47.767
I hope we don't get wet," Will said.

64
00:03:47.787 --> 00:03:49.288
"What makes it snow?"

65
00:03:50.084 --> 00:03:57.631
"Snowflakes form when a cloud's temperature is below freezing, so water vapor turns directly into ice without becoming

66
00:03:57.691 --> 00:03:59.012
drops of water first.

67
00:03:59.513 --> 00:04:04.377
The ice crystals give each snowflake a unique shape," QT answered.

68
00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:07.480
"Hail is another kind of precipitation.

69
00:04:08.001 --> 00:04:09.522
It forms in thunderclouds.

70
00:04:11.124 --> 00:04:15.688
Water droplets are carried by wind from warmer parts of the cloud into colder parts.

71
00:04:16.788 --> 00:04:22.392
The droplets freeze, and when they fall back through the warm parts of the cloud, the little balls

72
00:04:22.432 --> 00:04:26.154
of ice are covered by a layer of water condensing around them.

73
00:04:27.275 --> 00:04:32.979
If another wind carries the ice up again, that layer will freeze, making the hail bigger.

74
00:04:33.699 --> 00:04:37.802
After many cycles of this, hail can become as big as a baseball!"

75
00:04:37.822 --> 00:04:44.747
"I hope that thundercloud doesn't create such big hail," Will muttered, nervously watching the dark cloud.

76
00:04:45.247 --> 00:04:46.208
"It's coming this way!"

77
00:04:47.036 --> 00:04:48.017
"We'd better land.

78
00:04:48.397 --> 00:04:51.140
We don't want to be up here if that cloud produces lightning!

79
00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.983
Pull on that cord to release some hot air," QT instructed.

80
00:04:55.984 --> 00:04:59.186
Will pulled on the cord, and a vent at the top of the balloon opened.

81
00:04:59.627 --> 00:05:01.148
They began to descend slowly.

82
00:05:02.469 --> 00:05:03.750
"What causes lightning?"

83
00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:04.571
Will asked.

84
00:05:05.731 --> 00:05:10.996
"Well, when rain starts to fall from a thundercloud, it pulls some air down with it.

85
00:05:11.688 --> 00:05:17.633
The falling air and water rubs against the rising air and water, which creates electric charges.

86
00:05:18.414 --> 00:05:20.515
Lightning allows this energy to flow.

87
00:05:20.896 --> 00:05:24.759
The same basic thing happens when you touch a metal doorknob and feel a shock."

88
00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:29.943
Suddenly a lightning bolt escaped from the cloud, and thunder soon followed.

89
00:05:30.984 --> 00:05:35.728
Will tried not to seem scared, asking, "Why do we usually hear thunder after lightning?"

90
00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:41.621
"When lightning strikes, it heats the air around it to thirty thousand degrees Celsius!

91
00:05:42.222 --> 00:05:44.302
That makes the air expand very quickly.

92
00:05:45.002 --> 00:05:49.944
As it expands, it pushes the air around it, which creates a sound wave—thunder.

93
00:05:51.104 --> 00:05:55.265
We see lightning first because light travels much faster than sound."

94
00:05:55.745 --> 00:06:00.747
"I heard that you can measure how far away lightning is by counting the seconds between the lightning

95
00:06:00.887 --> 00:06:01.467
and thunder.

96
00:06:03.328 --> 00:06:07.783
If three seconds pass, the lightning struck about a kilometer away," Will said.

97
00:06:07.803 --> 00:06:12.326
"That's right, but we're almost to the ground!

98
00:06:12.727 --> 00:06:13.867
Get ready to land!"

99
00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:19.532
Will grabbed the side of the basket and closed his eyes.

100
00:06:20.093 --> 00:06:21.794
The basket shook as it touched the ground.

101
00:06:23.715 --> 00:06:27.659
Will opened one eye and saw his dad standing over him, gently shaking his shoulder.

102
00:06:27.679 --> 00:06:30.981
"Time to go, sleepyhead," he said.

103
00:06:31.001 --> 00:06:35.378
"I hope it's still storming," Will said as he got up.

104
00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:37.563
"Storms are really amazing, Dad!"
